ipa: simple: Let the tuning file set the exposure target

The AGC aims at a mean sample value in the middle of its five histogram
bins, which puts the mean of the linear image at about 40% of full
scale. Once the gamma curve is applied that is a bright picture: on the
Surface Pro 11's front camera in daylight, the mean of the output was
169 of 255 and most of a face lit from the window was above 245. The new
optional exposureTarget parameter of the Agc algorithm replaces that
middle value (1.3 to 5, default 2.5; a value out of range is clamped to
it, with a warning).

The AGC's thresholds and proportional gain were chosen for the middle
value; scale them with the target, so that a low target keeps the same
relative behaviour. A target of 1.4 is otherwise at most 0.4 away from
the lowest possible MSV of 1, below the large error threshold, and a dark
scene converges by small proportional steps only (half a minute in a
simulation of a dim room, four to six seconds with the scaling). The MSV
also rises far less than the brightness near the dark end, so square the
ratio when brightening from far below the target (two to three seconds).
Even scaled, a target of 1.25 or less never reaches the large error
threshold from a dark frame, and one of about 1.09 or less counts a black
frame as on target, hence the lower bound of 1.3.

The tuning files of both Surface Pro 11 cameras set 1.4, about a stop
darker than the default, the value chosen on the device for the front
camera from 1.0, 1.4, 1.6 and 1.8.
---
 src/ipa/simple/algorithms/agc.cpp | 66 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 src/ipa/simple/algorithms/agc.h   |  1 +
 src/ipa/simple/data/imx681.yaml   |  5 +++
 src/ipa/simple/data/ov13858.yaml  |  4 ++
 4 files changed, 70 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/ipa/simple/algorithms/agc.cpp b/src/ipa/simple/algorithms/agc.cpp
index 1f7275cb8..48d9d9ffa 100644
--- a/src/ipa/simple/algorithms/agc.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/simple/algorithms/agc.cpp
@@ -32,6 +32,12 @@ static constexpr unsigned int kExposureBinsCount = 5;
  */
 static constexpr float kExposureOptimal = kExposureBinsCount / 2.0;
 
+/*
+ * The lowest exposure target the AGC converges to from a dark frame (see
+ * Agc::init()).
+ */
+static constexpr double kExposureTargetMin = 1.3;
+
 /*
  * This implements the hysteresis for the exposure adjustment.
  * It is small enough to have the exposure close to the optimal, and is big
@@ -99,6 +105,31 @@ int Agc::init([[maybe_unused]] IPAContext &context, const ValueNode &tuningData)
 		maxAnalogueGain_.reset();
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The mean sample value the exposure aims at, on the scale of the
+	 * histogram bins (1 to kExposureBinsCount). The default, the middle of
+	 * the range, puts the mean of the linear image at about 40% of full
+	 * scale, which clips bright subjects once the gamma curve is applied.
+	 *
+	 * The MSV cannot drop below 1 (every sample in the darkest bin), and
+	 * the thresholds below scale with the target: a target of 1.25 or less
+	 * never reaches the large error threshold from a dark frame, and one of
+	 * about 1.09 or less counts a black frame as on target. A target out of
+	 * range is clamped to it.
+	 */
+	exposureTarget_ = tuningData["exposureTarget"].get<double>(kExposureOptimal);
+	if (!(exposureTarget_ >= kExposureTargetMin && exposureTarget_ <= kExposureBinsCount)) {
+		const double target = std::isnan(exposureTarget_)
+					      ? kExposureOptimal
+					      : std::clamp<double>(exposureTarget_, kExposureTargetMin,
+								   kExposureBinsCount);
+		LOG(IPASoftExposure, Warning)
+			<< "exposureTarget " << exposureTarget_ << " outside "
+			<< kExposureTargetMin << " to " << kExposureBinsCount
+			<< ", using " << target;
+		exposureTarget_ = target;
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -125,6 +156,11 @@ int Agc::configure(IPAContext &context, [[maybe_unused]] const IPAConfigInfo &co
 			<< " by the tuning file";
 	}
 
+	if (exposureTarget_ != kExposureOptimal)
+		LOG(IPASoftExposure, Info)
+			<< "Exposure target " << exposureTarget_
+			<< " by the tuning file";
+
 	context.activeState.agc.dgain = 1.0;
 	agc.dgainMax = maxDigitalGain_;
 
@@ -150,9 +186,19 @@ void Agc::updateExposure(IPAContext &context, IPAFrameContext &frameContext, dou
 	double &dgain = frameContext.agc.digitalGain;
 	const double dgainMax = context.configuration.agc.dgainMax;
 
-	double error = kExposureOptimal - exposureMSV;
+	double error = exposureTarget_ - exposureMSV;
+
+	/*
+	 * The thresholds and the proportional gain were chosen for the default
+	 * target in the middle of the histogram. Scale them with the target so
+	 * that a lower one keeps the same relative behaviour: a target of 1.4
+	 * is at most 0.4 away from the lowest possible MSV of 1, which never
+	 * reaches the large error threshold, and a dark scene would then only
+	 * converge by small proportional steps.
+	 */
+	const double scale = exposureTarget_ / kExposureOptimal;
 
-	if (std::abs(error) <= kExposureSatisfactory)
+	if (std::abs(error) <= kExposureSatisfactory * scale)
 		return;
 
 	/*
@@ -162,11 +208,19 @@ void Agc::updateExposure(IPAContext &context, IPAFrameContext &frameContext, dou
 	 * ratio; near it, apply a small proportional step.
 	 */
 	float factor;
-	if (std::abs(error) > kExpLargeError) {
-		factor = std::clamp(static_cast<float>(kExposureOptimal / std::max(exposureMSV, 0.1)),
-				    1.0f / kExpMaxJump, kExpMaxJump);
+	if (std::abs(error) > kExpLargeError * scale) {
+		double ratio = exposureTarget_ / std::max(exposureMSV, 0.1);
+
+		/*
+		 * Near the dark end most samples share the lowest bins, and the MSV
+		 * rises far less than the brightness: the ratio understates the
+		 * correction a dark scene needs, so square it when brightening.
+		 */
+		if (ratio > 1.0)
+			ratio *= ratio;
+		factor = std::clamp(static_cast<float>(ratio), 1.0f / kExpMaxJump, kExpMaxJump);
 	} else {
-		float step = std::clamp(static_cast<float>(error) * kExpProportionalGain,
+		float step = std::clamp(static_cast<float>(error / scale) * kExpProportionalGain,
 					-kExpMaxStep, kExpMaxStep);
 		factor = 1.0f + step;
 	}
